<section class="prose" aria-labelledby="stores">
  <h2 id="stores">What the CDC Assistant stores</h2>
  <p>
    The CDC Assistant is optional. The whole site works without it, and
    nothing below happens unless you open the assistant, ask it something,
    and then click 👍 or 👎 on an answer. Typing a question alone sends
    nothing: the assistant answers from a data file your browser has
    already downloaded.
  </p>
  <p>
    When you do click 👍 or 👎, your browser sends one record to the
    project's database (a Supabase Postgres table named
    <code>assistant_feedback</code>). It contains exactly these fields:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The last question you typed</strong>
      (<code>question</code>), cut to 2,000 characters. This is your own
      text, so please do not type passwords, connection strings or
      personal details into the assistant.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The topic the assistant matched</strong> to your question
      (<code>intent_id</code>). It can be empty when nothing matched.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your vote</strong> (<code>helpful</code>): yes for 👍, no for
      👎.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The time</strong> of the vote (<code>ts</code>).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A random ID</strong> (<code>id</code>) generated in your
      browser. It is not linked to you; it only lets a retried send be
      recognised as a duplicate.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The site's own code does not collect a name, an email address, an
    account, a cookie value or an IP address. The purpose is narrow: to see
    which questions the assistant answers badly so the answers can be
    improved.
  </p>
  <p>
    If sending fails, the vote (including the question text) waits in your
    browser and is retried on a later visit, for up to 14 days or 8 tries,
    then dropped.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="prose" aria-labelledby="playground">
  <h2 id="playground">The Change Feed Playground</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="{{ '/playground/' | url }}">Change Feed Playground</a> is a
    separate, optional tool that works differently from the rest of the
    site. It talks to the same Supabase project as the assistant, so please
    read this section before you type anything real into it. Use made-up
    data.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>When the page opens</strong>, it contacts the database to see
      whether it is reachable and, if it is, opens a live connection. That
      happens without you clicking anything, so the database host sees your
      IP address as soon as the playground loads. The code that does this is
      loaded from a CDN (see below).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Change events are public.</strong> When you insert, update or
      delete a row, or emit a snapshot, the event is added to a database
      table called <code>events</code>: a random ID (<code>id</code>), a
      timestamp (<code>ts_ms</code>), the operation (<code>op</code>), and the
      row values <em>as you typed them</em> (<code>after</code>, and
      <code>before</code> too while the "include before" box is ticked,
      which it is by default). Anyone can read that table and receives new
      events live, and other visitors' events can appear in your workspace
      too. Do not enter real names, emails or other personal data.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Shared scenarios are reachable by link.</strong> "Copy Share
      Link" stores a snapshot of your workspace
      (table layout, rows, events and comparator settings, up to about 1 MB)
      in a table called <code>scenarios</code> under a random ID. The table
      cannot be listed, but anyone who has the share link can open the
      snapshot.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Deleted after 30 days.</strong> A scheduled database job runs
      every day and deletes playground events and saved scenarios that are
      older than 30 days. Because the job runs once a day, a record can
      last up to about 31 days. When a scenario is deleted, its share link
      stops working, so a link you copy will expire after about 30 days. The
      30 days are counted from the server's time when the record is stored,
      so a device's clock cannot change it. Visitors' browsers can only add
      records (and, for events, read them): they cannot change or delete any,
      so you cannot delete your own events or scenarios sooner; you can ask
      for earlier removal (see "Asking about or deleting your entry").
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stays on your device:</strong> your workspace, your onboarding
      and template choices, feature toggles and a small buffer of usage
      counters are kept in local storage. The usage counters are not sent
      anywhere.
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="prose" aria-labelledby="newsletter">
  <h2 id="newsletter">The newsletter signup</h2>
  {% if newsletter.enabled %}
  <p>
    The <a href="{{ '/newsletter/' | url }}">newsletter</a> has a signup form
    on its page and in the footer, with one field: your email address. The
    newsletter is run by Buttondown, a separate company.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>What is sent:</strong> the email address you typed, plus what
      any request reveals to the receiving site (your IP address and browser
      details).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>To whom:</strong> Buttondown (<strong>buttondown.com</strong>),
      which then sends its own confirmation and newsletter emails. This site
      does not keep the address.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>When:</strong> only when you press Subscribe. The form is an
      ordinary web form: your browser sends it and you continue on
      Buttondown's site. Nothing is sent as you type, and the site loads no
      script from Buttondown. Buttondown's own pages, where you continue
      after pressing Subscribe, may set cookies under its privacy policy.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Buttondown handles the address under its own
    <a href="{{ newsletter.privacyUrl }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">privacy policy</a>.
    As the newsletter's sender, the maintainer, {{ author.name }}, can see
    the subscriber list in Buttondown.
  </p>
  <p>
    To unsubscribe, use the unsubscribe link Buttondown normally includes in
    its emails. You can also write to the maintainer on
    <a href="{{ author.advisoryUrl }}" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a> to ask
    about your address. The "Asking about or deleting your entry" section
    below is for anonymous assistant and playground entries, not for
    newsletter subscribers.
  </p>
  {% else %}
  <p>
    The newsletter signup is not open yet, so this site does not ask for or
    send an email address anywhere. This section will describe the signup
    when it opens.
  </p>
  {% endif %}
</section>

<section class="prose" aria-labelledby="device">
  <h2 id="device">What stays on your device</h2>
  <p>
    Your browser's local storage holds a few things that are never sent
    anywhere:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>the assistant's chat history (the last 20 messages);</li>
    <li>your reading progress and scorecard answers;</li>
    <li>
      your theme and reading-depth choices, and what you type into a few
      interactive tools.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Clearing the site's data in your browser settings removes all of it.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="prose" aria-labelledby="cookies">
  <h2 id="cookies">Cookies and analytics</h2>
  {% if analytics.goatcounterCode %}
  <p>
    The site's own code does not set cookies. It does count visits with
    GoatCounter, which sets no cookies either; see "Visit counts" below.
  </p>
  {% else %}
  <p>
    The site's own code does not set cookies or use analytics.
  </p>
  {% endif %}
  {% if newsletter.enabled %}
  <p>
    Separately, if you use the newsletter signup, the Buttondown page you continue to
    may set its own cookies.
  </p>
  {% endif %}
  <p>
    Some features load things from other companies, which can behave
    differently. If you click play on an embedded YouTube video, YouTube's
    player loads and may set its own cookies. Mermaid and Chart.js come
    from jsDelivr, which can see your IP address. The Change Feed
    Playground loads its Supabase library from jsDelivr and contacts
    Supabase when it opens. Details are under "Third-party requests".
  </p>
</section>

{% if analytics.goatcounterCode %}
<section class="prose" aria-labelledby="visit-counts">
  <h2 id="visit-counts">Visit counts</h2>
  <p>
    The site counts visits with <a href="https://www.goatcounter.com/" rel="noopener">GoatCounter</a>,
    a privacy-friendly analytics service. It sets no cookies and shows no
    consent banner, because it stores nothing in your browser and keeps no
    personal profile of you.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>What is counted</strong> when you open a page: the page path
      and title, the page you came from (the referrer), your browser name
      and version, your operating system, your screen width, your language,
      and your country, which GoatCounter works out from your IP address.
      Per GoatCounter, the results are stored as totals per day or hour, so
      the browser and country figures cannot be linked to each other or to
      you.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What is not kept:</strong> according to GoatCounter's privacy
      policy it does not store your IP address, your full browser
      identification string or any tracking ID. To avoid counting a
      repeat visit twice it holds a short-lived random string in its
      server's memory for up to 8 hours; that string is not written to its
      database. There is no cross-site tracking: the counts are not
      combined with data from other sites.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Who handles it:</strong> GoatCounter (goatcounter.com,
      operated by an individual in Ireland, with servers in Finland and
      Germany) processes the counts on the maintainer's behalf. Your browser
      loads a small script from <strong>gc.zgo.at</strong> and sends the
      count to this site's address at <strong>goatcounter.com</strong>, so
      those two hosts see your IP address as any host you contact does. The
      maintainer reads the totals in GoatCounter's dashboard.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do Not Track is respected.</strong> If your browser sends a
      Do Not Track signal, the script is not loaded and nothing is sent to
      either host. Content blockers that block the script have the same
      effect, and nothing on the site depends on it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>How long:</strong> the totals contain no personal data. By
      default nothing deletes them on a schedule; the
      maintainer can delete the whole GoatCounter site, and with it all the
      totals, at any time in GoatCounter's settings (GoatCounter says
      copies can remain in its backups for up to 30 days).
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>
{% endif %}

<section class="prose" aria-labelledby="third-parties">
  <h2 id="third-parties">Third-party requests</h2>
  <p>
    The pages are hosted on GitHub Pages, so GitHub receives each page
    request as any web host does. Fonts, styles and the site's own scripts
    are served from the site itself{% if analytics.goatcounterCode %}, except the visit-count script
    described below{% endif %}. A few features load something from
    another company, and any request to another host necessarily shows that
    host your IP address and browser details:
  </p>
  <ul>
    {% if analytics.goatcounterCode %}
    <li>
      <strong>gc.zgo.at</strong> and <strong>goatcounter.com</strong>: the
      visit-count script and the count itself, on every page except the
      playground; skipped when your browser sends Do Not Track (see "Visit
      counts").
    </li>
    {% endif %}
    <li>
      <strong>cdn.jsdelivr.net</strong> (the jsDelivr CDN): the Mermaid
      library on pages that draw diagrams, the Chart.js library on pages
      that show progress charts, and the Supabase browser library on the
      <a href="{{ '/playground/' | url }}">Change Feed Playground</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>img.youtube.com</strong>: a video thumbnail on the
      <a href="{{ '/tooling/' | url }}">Tooling</a> page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>www.youtube-nocookie.com</strong>: only if you click play on that
      video, which then loads YouTube's player.
    </li>
    {% if newsletter.enabled %}
    <li>
      <strong>buttondown.com</strong>: only when you press Subscribe on the
      newsletter form, as described under "The newsletter signup".
    </li>
    {% endif %}
    <li>
      <strong>Supabase</strong>, the database host: when you send an
      assistant vote, and whenever you open the Change Feed Playground, as
      described above.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These companies handle those requests under their own privacy
    policies, and Supabase may keep its own technical logs of the requests
    it receives. Links on the site to other websites are ordinary links:
    nothing is sent until you follow one.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="prose" aria-labelledby="retention">
  <h2 id="retention">How long we keep it</h2>
  <p>
    Assistant feedback records are kept for 12 months. A scheduled database
    job runs every day and deletes records older than that, so no one has
    to remember to do it. This policy took effect on 2026-10-09.
  </p>
  <p>
    This applies only to assistant feedback. Change Feed Playground events
    and saved scenarios are kept for a much shorter time: 30 days. A
    separate scheduled job runs every day and deletes playground events and
    saved scenarios older than that, so a record can last up to about 31
    days before it is removed, and a playground share link stops working
    once its scenario is deleted. The 30 days start from the database
    server's time when the record is stored; a visitor's device clock
    cannot change that. The playground also asks you, on the page,
    to use made-up data only. This policy took effect on 2026-10-09.
  </p>
  {% if newsletter.enabled %}
  <p>
    Newsletter subscribers are a separate case: Buttondown, not this site,
    keeps your address for as long as you are subscribed. What it keeps after
    you unsubscribe is set by its
    <a href="{{ newsletter.privacyUrl }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">privacy policy</a>
    and its own clean-up schedule.
  </p>
  {% endif %}
</section>

<section class="prose" aria-labelledby="who-sees">
  <h2 id="who-sees">Who can see it</h2>
  <p>
    Assistant feedback: the site's maintainer, {{ author.name }}, reads the
    records in the Supabase dashboard. Your browser is only allowed to add
    records: it cannot read any back, and visitors cannot see each other's
    questions. Supabase, as the database host, also has technical access to
    the service it runs.
  </p>
  <p>
    Change Feed Playground: events are readable by anyone, and other
    playground visitors see new events live while they are stored. A saved
    scenario is readable by anyone who has its link (see above) until it is
    deleted after 30 days. Visitors' browsers can only add playground
    records, and read events; they cannot change or delete any record.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="prose" aria-labelledby="delete">
  <h2 id="delete">Asking about or deleting your entry</h2>
  <p>
    You can ask what is stored or ask for an entry to be deleted. The
    private route is a message to the maintainer on
    <a href="{{ author.advisoryUrl }}" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a>. You can
    also
    <a href="https://github.com/{{ site.repository }}/issues/new">open a
    GitHub issue</a>, but issues are public: say only that you want to be
    contacted about a deletion, and send the details privately.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Never paste your question, a playground share link or any other
    private text into a public issue.</strong> A share link is a key: anyone
    who holds it can open that saved scenario.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because nothing identifies you, an entry can only be found if you can
    tell us the exact question you asked and roughly when you asked it.
    Without that we cannot tell which record is yours, and it will be
    deleted automatically within 12 months anyway (playground records
    within about 31 days). For a saved playground
    scenario, send the share link privately (LinkedIn message); for
    playground events, tell us roughly when you used it and what you
    entered.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="prose" aria-labelledby="changes">
  <h2 id="changes">Changes to this page</h2>
  <p>
    If what the site stores changes, this page is updated in the same
    change and the "Last updated" date at the top of this page moves. The full history is in the
    <a href="https://github.com/{{ site.repository }}/commits/main/src/privacy/index.njk">public
    Git log</a>.
  </p>
</section>
